## Increment 3 soundness guard (Codex high finding). The block_on-exit
## root-future drop force-roots a local named `__block_on_fut_*`. That name
## prefix is only a CHEAP FILTER scoping the rule to compiler-generated
## block_on roots; SOUNDNESS is structural — a local is force-rooted ONLY if
## it is never CONSUMED (never passed by-value to a call, stored into an
## aggregate, moved, or returned). A genuine block_on root is only borrowed.
##
## A (pathological) USER local that happens to share the `__block_on_fut_`
## name AND is consumed by-value must NOT be force-rooted — else its `def drop`
## would fire on a value already moved into the callee → double-free.
##
## Here `__block_on_fut_user` is a droppable `Crate` MOVED by value into `sink`.
## The local is CONSUMED by the call, so the structural check leaves it tainted
## (NOT force-rooted). If the force-root were name-only (unsound), drop WOULD
## fire at main's scope exit on a value already moved into `sink` → a
## double-free / crash. The clean exit (no crash) is the soundness proof.
## (Note: `box dropped` does not print at all — Ruxen's move-into-a-by-value
## param does not drop in the callee either; that's a pre-existing, orthogonal
## move-by-value leak, not what this pin guards. The pin guards ONLY that the
## force-root does not double-free a consumed `__block_on_fut_`-named local.)

class Crate
  tag: Int
  def init(t: Int)
    self.tag = t
  end
  def drop
    puts "box dropped tag=#{self.tag}"
  end
end

# Consumes its argument by value (owns it, drops it at this fn's scope exit).
def sink(b: Crate) -> nil
  puts "sink got tag=#{b.tag}"
end

def main
  let __block_on_fut_user = Crate.new(3)
  sink(__block_on_fut_user)
  puts "after sink"
end
